export const MH1={title:'The event and why mental health is health',hook:'Mental health shapes how we think, feel, learn, connect with others and handle stress. Yet many young people struggle quietly because they do not know the signs, feel ashamed or do not know where to turn. Mental Health Promotion asks your team to change that at your school through a peer-focused social media campaign built on prevention.',objectives:['Describe the Mental Health Promotion event, its two rounds and how it is scored.','Define mental health and explain the mental health continuum.','Describe the scope of youth mental health concerns using national data.','Explain why prevention and promotion matter.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Mental Health Promotion is a **team event for 2–6 members**. In **Round One**, the team creates and publishes a **social media campaign for peers** that builds and promotes **protective factors** (and/or addresses **risk factors**) around a selected youth mental health topic. The team works with a **Mental Health Consultant**—a school counselor, nurse, psychologist or community mental health professional (or the HOSA advisor if none is available)—and uploads **one combined PDF** by **May 15**: a summary presentation with a title slide, the completed **Mental Health Consultation Form**, reference pages and examples of the campaign with **public links**. Judges have **seven minutes** to review it.',
'In **Round Two**, every team that uploaded on time gives a **five-minute verbal presentation** to judges. A battery-powered device may show **parts of the campaign**, but no slides or other visual aids are allowed; notecards are optional. Round One is worth **115 points** and Round Two **105 points**, for **220 total**. Ties are broken by the highest-value rating-sheet sections.',
"Start early. Strong teams pick a consultant in the fall, finish their needs assessment and topic by winter, run the campaign for several weeks in early spring and spend the final weeks measuring results, building the PDF and rehearsing. Waiting until April leaves little time for consultant review or for peers to see and respond to the campaign.",
{t:'table',caption:'How the event is scored',head:['Part','What is judged','Points'],rows:[['Round One submission','Consultation form, summary, title page, references','30'],['Round One content','Risk/protective factors, understanding, effectiveness, peer education','60'],['Round One design','Appearance and organization, creativity and originality','25'],['Round Two content','Learning points, consultant, timeline, campaign use, why, factors, next steps','85'],['Round Two delivery','Voice, stage presence, diction, team participation','20']]}]],
['What mental health means',[
'The World Health Organization describes **mental health** as a state of well-being that lets people cope with the normal stresses of life, realize their abilities, learn and work well and contribute to their community. Mental health is more than the absence of a **mental illness**. A **mental health condition** (such as depression or an anxiety disorder) is a diagnosable condition that affects thinking, mood or behavior and interferes with daily life.',
'Many experts describe a **mental health continuum** from **thriving** to **surviving**, **struggling** and **in crisis**. Everyone moves along it. A person with a diagnosed condition can have periods of good mental health, and a person without a diagnosis can struggle during hard times. The phrase “**mental health IS health**” means we care for our minds the way we care for our bodies—through daily habits, prevention, early help and treatment when needed.',
order('Mental health continuum (from most to least well)',['Thriving','Surviving','Struggling','In crisis'])]],
['How young people are doing',[
'In 2021 the **U.S. Surgeon General** issued an advisory on **protecting youth mental health**, noting rising rates of depressive symptoms, anxiety and suicidal thoughts among young people over the previous decade and the added stress of the COVID-19 pandemic. CDC’s **Youth Risk Behavior Survey** found that in **2023** about **4 in 10** U.S. high school students reported **persistent feelings of sadness or hopelessness**, and about **1 in 5** had **seriously considered suicide** in the past year. Rates were higher among female students and LGBTQ+ students.',
'Most mental health conditions begin early: about **half** of lifetime conditions start by age **14**. Early support improves outcomes, yet many young people do not receive care because of **stigma**, cost, lack of providers or not knowing where to go. Schools, families and peers are often the first to notice changes.',
chk('About what fraction of U.S. high school students reported persistent sadness or hopelessness in the 2023 YRBS?',['About 4 in 10','About 1 in 100','About 9 in 10','About 1 in 50'],'CDC reported about 40% in 2023.')]],
['Prevention and promotion',[
'Public health uses three levels of prevention. **Promotion** and **primary prevention** build skills and environments that keep people well before problems start—such as teaching coping skills, building school connectedness and reducing bullying. **Secondary prevention** finds problems early, such as screening and teaching peers to recognize warning signs. **Tertiary prevention** supports recovery and prevents relapse for people already living with a condition.',
'Your campaign is a **promotion and prevention** project. It should not try to diagnose or treat anyone. Instead, it should help peers understand a topic, build **protective factors**, reduce **stigma** and know how and where to get help. That is why the event requires a consultant and safe, accurate information.',
sort('Level of prevention',['Promotion/primary','Secondary','Tertiary'],[['A campaign teaching stress-management skills',0],['A school depression screening day',1],['A support group for students in recovery from an eating disorder',2],['An anti-bullying peer pledge',0],['Teaching peers to notice warning signs and refer to a counselor',1]])]],
['Mental health and stigma',[
'**Stigma** is negative attitudes or beliefs about people with mental health conditions. **Public stigma** shows up as stereotypes and discrimination; **self-stigma** is when people believe those stereotypes about themselves; **structural stigma** is found in policies and systems. Stigma keeps people from talking about their feelings or seeking help.',
'Campaigns reduce stigma by sharing accurate information, using **respectful, person-first language** (“a person living with depression,” not “a depressive”), avoiding jokes or slang that trivialize conditions (“I’m so OCD”), highlighting recovery and help-seeking and featuring real voices with permission. Contact with people who share their experiences safely is one of the most effective ways to reduce stigma.',
sort('Stigma-reducing or stigmatizing?',['Reduces stigma','Stigmatizing'],[['“A person living with anxiety”',0],['“She’s crazy”',1],['Sharing that treatment works and recovery is possible',0],['Joking “I’m so depressed” about a bad grade',1],['Encouraging peers to talk to a trusted adult',0],['Calling someone “psycho”',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Starting the project',{a:{text:'Your team of four wants to begin Mental Health Promotion. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you do early?',choices:[['Identify a Mental Health Consultant and read the suggested resources','b','Correct.',true],['Start posting memes right away','b','Plan with your consultant first.',false]]},b:{text:'A teammate suggests a campaign that diagnoses classmates through a quiz. Is that appropriate?',choices:[['No—the campaign should promote protective factors and help-seeking, not diagnose','c','Correct.',true],['Yes, diagnosis is the goal','c','Diagnosis is for trained professionals.',false]]},c:{text:'You agree on a prevention focus and plan to upload one combined PDF by May 15.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Team of 2–6; Round One is a pre-judged peer social media campaign with a Mental Health Consultant and one combined PDF uploaded by May 15 (115 points; judges review for 7 minutes); Round Two is a 5-minute verbal presentation (105 points) with only campaign pieces shown on a device.','Mental health is a state of well-being that lets people cope, learn, work and contribute; people move along a continuum from thriving to in crisis; mental health is health.','Many youth struggle (2023 YRBS: about 4 in 10 persistently sad or hopeless; about 1 in 5 seriously considered suicide); campaigns focus on promotion and prevention, not diagnosis, and reduce stigma with accurate, person-first language.'],terms:[['Mental health','A state of well-being that allows people to cope, learn, work and contribute.'],['Stigma','Negative attitudes or beliefs about people with mental health conditions.'],['Protective factor','A characteristic or condition that lowers the likelihood of negative outcomes.']],mcqs:[q('How many members may be on a Mental Health Promotion team?','2–6','1','7–10','Exactly 3','Team event.'),q('How long is the Round Two presentation?','5 minutes','15 minutes','2 minutes','30 minutes','Verbal presentation.'),q('Which may be used during the Round Two presentation?','A device showing parts of the campaign','A PowerPoint presentation','Posters','A Prezi','No other visual aids.'),q('How long do judges have to review the Round One submission?','Seven minutes','Two minutes','One hour','Ten seconds','Plan content accordingly.'),q('What is the main focus of the campaign?','Protective and/or risk factors for a youth mental health topic','Diagnosing classmates','Selling a product','Advertising a clinic','Prevention focus.'),q('Which phrase is person-first language?','A person living with depression','A depressive','A psycho','A crazy person','Respectful wording.'),q('About half of lifetime mental health conditions begin by age:','14','40','60','5','Early onset.'),q('Who may serve as a Mental Health Consultant if no counselor is available?','The local HOSA advisor','A friend from another school','A celebrity','No one','Per the guidelines.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain what “mental health IS health” means and why a prevention-focused peer campaign is an appropriate way to support youth mental health at your school.',rubric:[['Definition',['well-being','cope','stress','learn','work','contribute','not just absence']],['Continuum and need',['continuum','thriving','struggling','crisis','yrbs','sadness','hopelessness','surgeon general']],['Prevention',['promotion','prevention','protective factors','risk factors','early','help-seeking']],['Campaign role',['peers','stigma','accurate','consultant','not diagnose','resources']]],model:'“Mental health IS health” means that our emotional, psychological and social well-being matters as much as our physical health and deserves the same daily care, prevention and treatment. Mental health is a state of well-being that lets people cope with normal stress, realize their abilities, learn and work well and contribute to their community; it is more than the absence of a mental illness. Everyone moves along a continuum from thriving to surviving, struggling and in crisis, so even students without a diagnosis can struggle. National data show the need: the Surgeon General’s 2021 advisory described rising youth mental health concerns, and CDC’s 2023 Youth Risk Behavior Survey found that about 4 in 10 high school students felt persistently sad or hopeless. A prevention-focused peer campaign fits this need because promotion and primary prevention build protective factors—such as coping skills and connection—before problems become severe, and secondary prevention helps peers recognize warning signs and seek help early. Students often listen to peers, and social media reaches them where they spend time. By working with a Mental Health Consultant, sharing accurate information, using respectful language to reduce stigma and pointing to trusted resources like counselors and the 988 Lifeline, the campaign supports students without trying to diagnose or treat anyone.'},sources:[RULES,SG,WHO_MH,CDC_YRBS]};

export const MH2={title:'Risk and protective factors',hook:'Two students face the same stressful semester. One has a close friend, a coach who checks in and a way to unwind; the other has none of these. Their outcomes may differ, not because one is stronger, but because of the factors surrounding them. Risk and protective factors are the heart of your campaign—judges score them in both rounds.',objectives:['Define risk and protective factors for youth mental health.','Classify factors at the individual, family, school and community levels.','Explain adverse childhood experiences and resilience.','Choose protective factors a peer campaign can realistically build.'],sections:[
['Defining risk and protective factors',[
'**Risk factors** are characteristics at the biological, psychological, family, community or cultural level that are associated with a **higher likelihood of negative outcomes**. **Protective factors** are characteristics associated with a **lower likelihood** of negative outcomes or that **reduce the impact** of risk factors. Risk and protective factors are not destiny: they shift probabilities, and many young people with several risk factors thrive.',
'Factors often **cluster**—a student facing poverty may also face housing instability and community violence—and they **interact**. Many protective factors work across several outcomes: strong relationships, for example, protect against depression, substance use and suicide. That is why prevention programs often focus on building **protective factors** rather than targeting one problem at a time.',
"Think of risk and protective factors as weights on a scale. Adding protective factors—like a supportive coach, a regular sleep schedule or a club where a student belongs—can balance out risks a young person cannot control, such as family stress or a community crisis. Your campaign adds weight to the protective side.",
sort('Risk or protective factor?',['Risk factor','Protective factor'],[['Being bullied',0],['A trusted adult at school',1],['Witnessing violence at home',0],['Healthy coping and problem-solving skills',1],['Social isolation',0],['Participation in extracurricular activities',1]])]],
['The social-ecological model',[
'The **social-ecological model** shows that health is shaped at several levels. **Individual** factors include genetics, temperament, coping skills, self-esteem and sleep. **Relationship (family and peer)** factors include family support, parental monitoring, friendships and peer pressure. **School** factors include **school connectedness**, a positive climate, safety and supportive teachers. **Community and society** factors include access to mental health care, neighborhood safety, discrimination, poverty and social media environments.',
'A campaign can work at several levels at once—for example, teaching an individual coping skill, encouraging peers to check on friends and promoting a school club. Explaining which level your factors belong to shows judges a deeper understanding.',
match('Factor and level',[['Coping skills','Individual'],['Family support','Relationship'],['Peer friendships','Relationship'],['Feeling connected to school','School'],['Access to counselors','Community'],['Neighborhood violence','Community']])]],
['Key protective factors for youth',[
'Research and CDC guidance highlight **connectedness**—the sense that people at school and at home care about you—as one of the strongest protective factors for adolescents. Students who feel connected to school are less likely to report persistent sadness, suicidal thoughts and substance use. Other protective factors include **supportive relationships with adults**, **positive friendships**, **coping and emotional regulation skills**, **problem-solving skills**, **healthy sleep, nutrition and physical activity**, **a sense of purpose** and **access to care**.',
'Protective factors are **buildable**. Peers can invite others to activities, teach and model coping strategies, share how to reach a counselor and celebrate help-seeking. These are realistic goals for a student campaign.',
flip('Protective factors a campaign can build',[['Connectedness','Feeling cared about at school and home'],['Trusted adults','Knowing whom to talk to'],['Coping skills','Breathing, journaling, movement'],['Friendship','Checking in on peers'],['Healthy routines','Sleep, nutrition, activity'],['Help-seeking','Knowing how to reach support']])]],
['Adverse childhood experiences and resilience',[
'**Adverse childhood experiences (ACEs)** are potentially traumatic events in childhood, such as abuse, neglect, witnessing violence or growing up with household substance use, mental illness or incarceration. CDC reports that about **64%** of U.S. adults experienced at least one ACE and about **1 in 6** experienced four or more. ACEs are linked to later depression, substance use and chronic disease. **Positive childhood experiences**—such as feeling able to talk with family and having supportive adults—help buffer these effects.',
'**Resilience** is the ability to adapt and recover from adversity. It is not a fixed trait; it grows through relationships, skills and supportive environments. Campaigns can promote resilience without asking anyone to disclose trauma online. Never encourage peers to share personal traumatic experiences publicly.',
chk('Which best describes resilience?',['The ability to adapt and recover from adversity, built through relationships and skills','A fixed trait you are born with','Never feeling stressed','Ignoring problems'],'Resilience can be strengthened.')]],
['Choosing factors for your campaign',[
'Choose factors with your consultant based on **needs** (what your school data or counselor say students face) and **interest** (what your team cares about). The guidelines give examples: a school where many students self-harm, with bullying and peer conflict as risk factors, might focus on **navigating bullying and resolving conflict**; a school where isolated students show signs of depression might focus on **engagement and connection through extracurricular activities**.',
'Good factors are **specific**, **supported by evidence** and **changeable** by a peer campaign. “Mental health” is too broad; “school connectedness to protect against depression” or “healthy coping skills for test anxiety” is focused. Thread the factors through **every** post so judges can see them clearly.',
sort('Focused enough for a campaign?',['Focused','Too broad'],[['Healthy coping skills for test anxiety',0],['All of mental health',1],['Connection through clubs to reduce isolation',0],['Everything about teenagers',1],['Bystander skills to reduce bullying',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Picking a factor',{a:{text:'Your consultant shares that many ninth graders report feeling lonely and disconnected. (Scenario invented for practice.) Which protective factor fits?',choices:[['School connectedness through clubs and peer welcome activities','b','Correct.',true],['Genetics','b','Genetics cannot be changed by a campaign.',false]]},b:{text:'How do you show this factor in the campaign?',choices:[['Every post links back to connection—club spotlights, “invite a friend” challenges and how to reach a counselor','c','Correct. Thread it throughout.',true],['Mention it once in the final post','c','Judges look for the factor throughout.',false]]},c:{text:'Your consultant approves the focus and helps you find evidence about connectedness.',end:true}})]],
["Risk and protection in a digital world",["Social media can be both a **risk** and a **protective** factor. In 2023 the Surgeon General issued a separate advisory on **social media and youth mental health**, noting that nearly all teens use social media and that heavy use is linked with poorer sleep, body-image concerns, exposure to harmful content and **cyberbullying**. Constant comparison with edited images can lower self-esteem.","Social media can also **connect** isolated young people with supportive communities, share accurate health information and help peers find resources. Your campaign can model **healthy use**: setting time limits, turning off notifications at night, curating feeds to follow positive accounts, taking breaks and reporting bullying.","Because your campaign lives on social media, it is a chance to show what positive, supportive content looks like. Think about how your posts make viewers feel—hopeful, connected and informed—rather than anxious or ashamed.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Risk or protective use of social media?","buckets":["Risk","Protective"],"items":[["Scrolling for hours late at night",0],["Following accounts that teach coping skills",1],["Comparing yourself to edited photos",0],["Joining an online group for students with shared interests",1],["Receiving hurtful comments",0],["Turning off notifications before bed",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Risk factors raise and protective factors lower the likelihood of negative outcomes; they cluster and interact but are not destiny.','The social-ecological model places factors at individual, relationship, school and community levels; connectedness, trusted adults, coping skills, friendships, healthy routines and help-seeking are buildable protective factors.','About 64% of adults report at least one ACE and 1 in 6 report four or more; resilience can be built; choose specific, evidence-based, changeable factors with your consultant and thread them through every post.'],terms:[['Risk factor','A characteristic linked to a higher likelihood of negative outcomes.'],['School connectedness','A student’s belief that adults and peers at school care about them.'],['Adverse childhood experience','A potentially traumatic event that occurs in childhood.']],mcqs:[q('Which is a protective factor?','A trusted adult at school','Being bullied','Social isolation','Community violence','Lowers risk.'),q('Which is a risk factor?','Witnessing violence at home','Strong friendships','Coping skills','School connectedness','Raises risk.'),q('Feeling that people at school care about you is called:','School connectedness','Self-stigma','Resilience only','An ACE','Strong protective factor.'),q('About what percentage of U.S. adults report at least one ACE?','About 64%','About 5%','About 100%','About 20%','CDC estimate.'),q('Resilience is:','The ability to adapt and recover, which can be built','A fixed trait','Never feeling sad','The same as stigma','Grows with support.'),q('In the social-ecological model, family support is at the:','Relationship level','Individual level','Community level','Policy level only','Family and peers.'),q('Which campaign focus is specific enough?','Healthy coping skills for test anxiety','All of mental health','Everything about teens','The whole world','Focused.'),q('How often should risk and protective factors appear in your campaign?','Throughout every part','Once at the end','Never','Only in the references','Judges look for them.')],frq:{prompt:'Define risk and protective factors, give examples at two levels of the social-ecological model and explain how your team would choose factors for a peer social media campaign.',rubric:[['Definitions',['risk factor','higher likelihood','protective factor','lower likelihood','reduce','impact']],['Levels',['individual','relationship','family','peer','school','community','connectedness']],['Choosing',['consultant','needs','data','interest','specific','evidence','changeable']],['Application',['thread','every post','campaign','peers','example','bullying','isolation']]],model:'Risk factors are characteristics of a person or their environment that are associated with a higher likelihood of negative outcomes, while protective factors are characteristics associated with a lower likelihood of those outcomes or that reduce the impact of risk. At the individual level, poor sleep and limited coping skills are risk factors, while healthy coping strategies like deep breathing, exercise and problem-solving are protective. At the school level, bullying and an unsafe climate are risk factors, while school connectedness—feeling that teachers and peers care about you—is one of the strongest protective factors against persistent sadness and suicidal thoughts. To choose factors, our team would meet with our Mental Health Consultant and review what students at our school face, such as counselor observations or survey data, and combine that need with our team’s interests. We would pick factors that are specific, supported by evidence and changeable by a peer campaign. For example, if our counselor reported that many freshmen feel isolated, we would focus on building connectedness through clubs and peer welcome activities as a protective factor against depression. We would then thread that factor through every post—club spotlights, “invite a friend” challenges and reminders about how to reach a counselor—so judges can clearly see it as the focus of the campaign.'},sources:[RULES,SAMHSA_MH,CDC_ACES,SG]};

export const MH3={title:'Common youth mental health concerns',hook:'Stress before a big test is normal. Weeks of hopelessness, panic that keeps a student home from school or skipping meals to control weight are not. Your campaign needs accurate, age-appropriate information about the topic you choose. This lesson introduces common concerns, their warning signs and how they are treated.',objectives:['Distinguish everyday stress from signs of a mental health condition.','Describe anxiety, depression, eating disorders, substance use and self-harm in youth.','Recognize warning signs that someone needs help.','Explain that mental health conditions are treatable.'],sections:[
['Stress and when it becomes a problem',[
'**Stress** is the body’s response to demands or threats. Short-term stress can help you focus before a performance. **Chronic** stress—from academic pressure, family conflict, discrimination, money worries or social media—can affect sleep, mood, concentration and physical health. Healthy coping includes sleep, physical activity, time outdoors, talking with others, breathing techniques, breaking tasks into steps and limiting screen time before bed.',
'A concern may be a **mental health condition** when symptoms are **intense**, **last weeks**, and **interfere with daily life**—school, relationships, activities or self-care. Only trained professionals diagnose conditions. Peers can notice changes and encourage someone to talk to a trusted adult.',
"Changes to watch for include pulling away from friends, dropping grades, losing interest in activities, sleeping much more or less, eating much more or less, increased irritability and talking about feeling hopeless or worthless. One bad day is normal; a pattern that lasts is a reason to check in.",
sort('Everyday stress or sign to get help?',['Everyday stress','Sign to get help'],[['Nervous the night before a test',0],['Feeling hopeless almost every day for a month',1],['Frustrated after an argument, then feeling better',0],['Panic attacks that keep a student home from school',1],['Skipping meals and exercising excessively to lose weight',1],['Tired after a long practice',0]])]],
['Anxiety and depression',[
'**Anxiety disorders** are among the most common conditions in youth. They involve worry or fear that is **excessive** and **hard to control**—such as generalized anxiety, social anxiety, panic disorder and specific phobias. Signs include constant worry, restlessness, trouble sleeping, stomachaches or headaches, avoiding situations and panic attacks (racing heart, shortness of breath, dizziness).',
'**Depression** is more than sadness. Signs lasting **two weeks or more** include persistent sadness or irritability, **loss of interest** in activities, changes in sleep or appetite, fatigue, trouble concentrating, feelings of worthlessness or guilt and thoughts of death or suicide. In teens, depression often shows as **irritability**. Both anxiety and depression are **treatable** with therapy (such as cognitive behavioral therapy), sometimes medication and support.',
match('Sign and likely concern',[['Constant worry that is hard to control','Anxiety'],['Loss of interest in favorite activities','Depression'],['Sudden racing heart and fear of dying','Panic attack'],['Avoiding speaking in class from fear of judgment','Social anxiety'],['Persistent irritability and fatigue for weeks','Depression'],['Treatment that changes thought patterns','Cognitive behavioral therapy']])]],
['Eating disorders, substance use and self-harm',[
'**Eating disorders**—such as anorexia nervosa, bulimia nervosa, binge-eating disorder and avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder—are serious conditions involving disturbed eating and often intense concern about weight or shape. Warning signs include extreme dieting, skipping meals, secretive eating, compulsive exercise and dramatic weight changes. Campaigns should **avoid numbers** (weights, calories) and “before and after” images, which can be harmful.',
'**Substance use**—including alcohol, cannabis, nicotine vaping and misused prescription drugs—can harm the developing brain and is linked with anxiety and depression. **Self-harm** (intentionally hurting oneself, such as cutting) is often a way of coping with overwhelming emotions; it is a sign that someone needs support, even when they are not suicidal. Never share images or methods of self-harm.',
sort('Safe campaign content?',['Safe to include','Avoid'],[['Where to find support for eating concerns',0],['Calorie counts and goal weights',1],['How vaping affects the teen brain',0],['Photos of self-harm injuries',1],['Healthy ways to cope with strong emotions',0],['Before-and-after weight-loss photos',1]])]],
['Suicide warning signs',[
'**Suicide** is a leading cause of death among young people. Warning signs include **talking about wanting to die** or being a burden, **looking for ways** to end one’s life, **withdrawing** from friends and activities, **giving away belongings**, extreme mood swings, **increased substance use**, reckless behavior and saying goodbye. A sudden calm after deep depression can also be a warning sign.',
'If you are worried about someone, **ask directly**: “Are you thinking about suicide?” Asking does not put the idea in someone’s head; it shows you care and opens the door to help. **Stay with them**, remove access to lethal means if it is safe, and **connect them to help**—a trusted adult, a counselor or the **988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline** (call or text **988**). If someone is in immediate danger, call **911**.',
order('If you are worried about a friend',['Notice the warning signs','Ask directly about suicide','Listen without judgment','Do not keep it a secret','Connect to a trusted adult, counselor or 988','Call 911 if there is immediate danger'])]],
['Treatment and recovery',[
'Mental health conditions are **common** and **treatable**. Treatments include **psychotherapy** (talk therapy such as cognitive behavioral therapy and dialectical behavior therapy), **medication** when appropriate, **family therapy**, **school supports** and **peer support**. Many people recover fully or learn to manage symptoms well. Early help leads to better outcomes.',
'Professionals who help include **school counselors**, **school psychologists**, **school social workers**, **school nurses**, **pediatricians**, **psychiatrists**, **psychologists**, **licensed counselors** and **therapists**. Your campaign can explain how students at your school can reach these people—for example, how to schedule time with a counselor.',
"Many students do not know that school counseling is free and confidential within limits, or that they can ask a parent or doctor for a referral. Explaining these simple facts removes barriers to help-seeking.",
match('Professional and role',[['School counselor','Supports students’ academic and emotional needs'],['School psychologist','Assessment and mental health support at school'],['Psychiatrist','Medical doctor who can prescribe medication'],['Therapist','Provides talk therapy'],['Pediatrician','Screens for concerns during checkups'],['988 Lifeline','24/7 crisis support by call, text or chat']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Choosing a topic',{a:{text:'Your consultant says many students report test anxiety. (Scenario invented for practice.) Which campaign angle is best?',choices:[['Coping strategies and study routines as protective factors, plus how to reach a counselor','b','Correct.',true],['A quiz that tells students whether they have an anxiety disorder','b','Avoid diagnosing.',false]]},b:{text:'A teammate wants to post a dramatic video of someone having a panic attack. What should you consider?',choices:[['Whether it is accurate, respectful and helpful—and get the consultant’s input','c','Correct.',true],['More drama always means more views, so post it','c','Content must be safe and accurate.',false]]},c:{text:'You choose a calm video demonstrating box breathing and where to find help.',end:true}})]],
["Myths and facts about mental health",["Myths fuel stigma and keep people from seeking help. **Myth:** “Mental health problems are a sign of weakness.” **Fact:** they result from a mix of biology, experiences and environment, and anyone can be affected. **Myth:** “Teens are just being dramatic.” **Fact:** many mental health conditions begin in adolescence and deserve to be taken seriously.","**Myth:** “Talking about suicide makes people more likely to do it.” **Fact:** asking directly and caringly can open the door to help. **Myth:** “People don’t recover from mental illness.” **Fact:** treatment works, and many people recover or manage symptoms well. **Myth:** “Therapy is only for serious problems.” **Fact:** counseling can help with everyday stress, too.","A **myth-versus-fact** series is a popular, effective campaign format. Make sure every fact is supported by a reputable source such as NIMH, SAMHSA or CDC, and review it with your consultant.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Myth or fact?","buckets":["Myth","Fact"],"items":[["Mental health conditions are a sign of weakness",0],["Treatment for depression and anxiety works",1],["Asking about suicide puts the idea in someone’s head",0],["Many mental health conditions begin in adolescence",1],["Only adults have mental health conditions",0],["Counseling can help with everyday stress",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['A concern may be a condition when symptoms are intense, last weeks and interfere with daily life; only professionals diagnose; peers notice and connect to help.','Anxiety: excessive, hard-to-control worry and panic; depression: two or more weeks of sadness or irritability, loss of interest, sleep and appetite changes; eating disorders, substance use and self-harm need support—avoid numbers, methods and images.','Suicide warning signs include talking about dying, withdrawing and giving things away; ask directly, stay, connect to a trusted adult, counselor or 988 (call or text), or 911 for immediate danger; conditions are common and treatable.'],terms:[['Anxiety disorder','A condition with excessive, hard-to-control worry or fear.'],['Depression','A mood condition with persistent sadness or loss of interest for two weeks or more.'],['988 Lifeline','The national Suicide & Crisis Lifeline reached by calling or texting 988.']],mcqs:[q('How long do depression symptoms usually last before diagnosis?','Two weeks or more','One hour','One day','Ten years','Persistent symptoms.'),q('In teens, depression often shows as:','Irritability','Constant laughter','Perfect sleep','Increased energy only','Not just sadness.'),q('Which number reaches the Suicide & Crisis Lifeline?','988','411','311','123','Call or text.'),q('Does asking someone directly about suicide put the idea in their head?','No','Yes','Only for adults','Only online','Asking shows care.'),q('Which content should an eating disorder campaign avoid?','Calorie counts and goal weights','Where to find support','Encouraging talking to a trusted adult','Healthy coping','Numbers can harm.'),q('Which therapy changes unhelpful thought patterns?','Cognitive behavioral therapy','Surgery','Antibiotics','Physical therapy','CBT.'),q('Which professional can prescribe medication?','Psychiatrist','School counselor','Peer','Coach','Medical doctor.'),q('What should you do if a friend is in immediate danger?','Call 911','Wait until tomorrow','Keep it a secret','Post about it','Emergency.')],frq:{prompt:'Choose one youth mental health concern, describe its warning signs and treatments and explain how a peer campaign could share information about it safely.',rubric:[['Concern and signs',['anxiety','depression','warning signs','two weeks','worry','interest','sleep','irritability']],['Treatment',['treatable','therapy','cognitive behavioral','medication','counselor','recovery']],['Safe sharing',['accurate','no diagnosis','avoid','numbers','methods','images','consultant']],['Help-seeking',['trusted adult','counselor','988','911','reach','resources']]],model:'Depression is a common, treatable mood condition in teens. Warning signs that last two weeks or more include persistent sadness or irritability—teens often seem irritable rather than sad—loss of interest in activities they used to enjoy, changes in sleep or appetite, fatigue, trouble concentrating, feelings of worthlessness and sometimes thoughts of death or suicide. Depression is treated with psychotherapy such as cognitive behavioral therapy, which helps people change unhelpful thought patterns and build coping skills, sometimes medication prescribed by a psychiatrist or pediatrician, and support from family and school. Many young people recover, especially when they get help early. A peer campaign could share this information safely by focusing on protective factors like connection, sleep and physical activity and on recognizing changes in friends, rather than offering quizzes that diagnose. We would work with our Mental Health Consultant to check accuracy, use person-first language, avoid graphic content and never describe suicide methods. Every post would include how to get help: talking to a trusted adult, scheduling time with a school counselor, calling or texting 988 for the Suicide & Crisis Lifeline and calling 911 in an emergency. Sharing stories of help-seeking and recovery with permission would also reduce stigma and show that treatment works.'},sources:[RULES,NIMH_YOUTH,LIFELINE,JED]};

export const MH4={title:'Supporting a peer and connecting to help',hook:'Most young people who struggle tell a friend before they tell an adult. That makes peers powerful—but only if they know what to say, what not to say and when to bring in help. Teaching peers these skills is one of the most valuable things a campaign can do, and it is grounded in the event’s suggested resources.',objectives:['Describe how to start a supportive conversation with a peer.','Apply the five Be There golden rules.','Know your role and the limits of peer support.','Identify school, community and crisis resources.'],sections:[
['Noticing and starting the conversation',[
'Notice **changes**: a friend who stops coming to practice, posts hopeless messages, seems unusually angry or withdrawn, or stops caring about things they loved. Choose a **private, low-pressure** time to talk—during a walk or a car ride often feels easier than face-to-face across a table. Start with what you have noticed and why you care: “I’ve noticed you haven’t been yourself lately, and I care about you. How are you really doing?”',
'Be patient. Your friend may not want to talk at first. Let them know you are there whenever they are ready. Check in again later. Small, consistent actions—sending a message, sitting together at lunch, inviting them to an activity—show care and build connection.',
"If your friend does not want to talk, respect that, but stay connected. Let them know you will check in again, invite them to do something low-pressure and remind them of resources. Sometimes the conversation that matters happens days later, after they know you are not going away.",
order('Starting a supportive conversation',['Notice changes in your friend','Choose a private, relaxed time','Say what you have noticed and that you care','Ask how they are really doing','Listen patiently','Check in again later'])]],
['The Be There golden rules',[
'**Be There**, a free online certificate program listed in the event resources, teaches five **golden rules** for supporting someone who is struggling: **say what you see**, **show you care**, **hear them out**, **know your role** and **connect to help**. These rules help peers start conversations, offer support and recognize when professional help is needed.',
'**Say what you see** means describing specific changes without judging. **Show you care** means making it clear you are there for them. **Hear them out** means listening without interrupting, fixing or minimizing. **Know your role** means recognizing that you are a friend, not a therapist. **Connect to help** means guiding them toward a trusted adult, counselor or crisis line.',
match('Golden rule and example',[['Say what you see','“I’ve noticed you’ve been skipping lunch.”'],['Show you care','“I’m here for you, no matter what.”'],['Hear them out','Listening without interrupting or fixing'],['Know your role','You are a friend, not a therapist'],['Connect to help','Walking with them to the counselor'],['All five together','Be There golden rules']])]],
['Listening well and what to avoid',[
'**Active listening** means giving full attention, making eye contact, nodding, reflecting back what you hear (“It sounds like you’ve been really overwhelmed”) and asking open questions (“What has that been like?”). **Validate** feelings: “That makes sense,” “That sounds really hard.” Validation does not mean you agree with everything; it means you accept that their feelings are real.',
'Avoid **minimizing** (“It’s not a big deal”), **comparing** (“I had it worse”), **lecturing** or jumping to **advice** too soon, **making jokes** about their feelings and **promising to keep everything secret**. If they share thoughts of harming themselves, you **cannot** keep that secret—getting help is a way of caring.',
sort('Helpful or unhelpful response?',['Helpful','Unhelpful'],[['“That sounds really hard. I’m glad you told me.”',0],['“Just think positive.”',1],['“What has that been like for you?”',0],['“Other people have it worse.”',1],['“I can’t keep this secret, but I’ll go with you to get help.”',0],['“You’re overreacting.”',1]])]],
['Knowing your role and caring for yourself',[
'Peers can listen, encourage and connect, but they **cannot** diagnose, treat or be someone’s only support. Bring in a **trusted adult**—a parent, teacher, coach, school counselor or nurse—when a friend’s struggles last, get worse, affect daily life or involve any risk of harm. Telling an adult is not betrayal; it is protection.',
'Supporting someone can be heavy. **Take care of yourself**: set boundaries, talk to your own trusted adult, keep up healthy routines and remember that you are not responsible for fixing your friend. Your campaign team should also check in with one another, especially when working on difficult topics.',
chk('A friend tells you they have been thinking about ending their life and asks you not to tell anyone. What should you do?',['Tell a trusted adult or contact 988 even though they asked you not to','Keep the secret to protect the friendship','Post about it online','Wait a few weeks to see if it passes'],'Safety comes before secrecy.')]],
['Resources at school, in the community and in crisis',[
'**School resources** include counselors, school psychologists, social workers, nurses and trusted teachers; many schools also have anonymous reporting apps. **Community resources** include pediatricians, community mental health centers, youth programs and faith leaders. **National resources** include the **988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline** (call, text or chat **988**, 24/7), **Crisis Text Line** (text **HOME** to **741741**) and the **Trevor Project** for LGBTQ+ youth. In an emergency, call **911**.',
'Your campaign should make resources **specific and easy to act on**: the counselor’s office location, how to book an appointment, the 988 number on every post and what happens when you reach out. Check every resource with your consultant to make sure it is current and appropriate for your school.',
match('Resource and how to reach it',[['988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline','Call, text or chat 988'],['Crisis Text Line','Text HOME to 741741'],['School counselor','Visit the counseling office or book online'],['Immediate danger','Call 911'],['Trevor Project','Support for LGBTQ+ youth'],['Trusted adult','Parent, teacher, coach or nurse']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('A worried text',{a:{text:'A friend texts late at night: “I don’t see the point anymore.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Respond with care, ask directly if they are thinking about suicide and connect them to 988 or a trusted adult','b','Correct.',true],['Ignore it until morning','b','Take it seriously now.',false]]},b:{text:'They say they are safe right now but feel hopeless. What next?',choices:[['Stay in touch, encourage them to text 988 tonight and tell a trusted adult in the morning','c','Correct.',true],['Promise never to tell anyone','c','Safety comes first.',false]]},c:{text:'The next morning you walk with your friend to the counselor’s office. You also talk to your own trusted adult about how you feel.',end:true}})]],
["Supporting friends online",["Many young people express distress **online**—through posts, stories or private messages. If a friend posts something worrying, **reach out privately** rather than commenting publicly: “I saw your post and I’m worried about you. Can we talk?” If the post suggests they may harm themselves, contact a **trusted adult** or **988** right away, and call **911** if there is immediate danger.","Most platforms have **reporting tools** for posts about self-harm or suicide, which can connect the person with support resources. Use them in addition to—not instead of—telling a trusted adult. Do not share screenshots of a friend’s distress publicly.","Supporting friends online can be draining. It is okay to set **digital boundaries**, such as not answering messages late at night when you are exhausted, as long as you make sure your friend knows how to reach crisis support, like texting 988, at any hour.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"If a friend posts something worrying","steps":["Take it seriously","Reach out privately","Ask how they are and listen","Tell a trusted adult or contact 988","Use the platform’s reporting tool","Call 911 if there is immediate danger"]}]]
],takeaways:['Notice changes, choose a private time, say what you see and why you care, ask how they are really doing, listen and check in again.','Be There golden rules: say what you see, show you care, hear them out, know your role, connect to help; listen actively and validate; avoid minimizing, comparing, lecturing and promising secrecy about harm.','Peers listen and connect but do not diagnose or treat; tell a trusted adult; resources include counselors, 988 (call, text or chat), Crisis Text Line (text HOME to 741741) and 911; care for yourself too.'],terms:[['Active listening','Giving full attention, reflecting back and asking open questions.'],['Validation','Accepting and acknowledging someone’s feelings as real.'],['Trusted adult','A safe adult who can help, such as a parent, counselor or teacher.']],mcqs:[q('Which is one of the Be There golden rules?','Say what you see','Keep every secret','Fix the problem yourself','Ignore changes','Five rules.'),q('Which response validates feelings?','“That sounds really hard.”','“Just get over it.”','“Others have it worse.”','“Stop being dramatic.”','Acknowledge feelings.'),q('What should you do if a friend shares thoughts of self-harm and asks for secrecy?','Tell a trusted adult or contact 988','Keep it secret','Post about it','Ignore it','Safety first.'),q('How do you reach the Crisis Text Line?','Text HOME to 741741','Call 411','Email the school','Text 911 only','24/7 text support.'),q('“Know your role” means:','You are a friend, not a therapist','You must diagnose','You should handle everything alone','You should avoid your friend','Connect to help.'),q('Which is active listening?','Reflecting back what you hear','Interrupting with advice','Checking your phone','Changing the subject','Full attention.'),q('Which is a sign a friend may need support?','Withdrawing from activities they used to enjoy','Getting a good grade','Laughing at a joke','Going to practice','Notice changes.'),q('Why should supporters care for themselves?','Supporting someone can be emotionally heavy','It is not necessary','To avoid their friend','To win the event','Set boundaries.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how a student can support a peer who seems to be struggling, using the Be There golden rules, and describe when and how to connect them to help.',rubric:[['Noticing and starting',['notice','changes','private','care','how are you']],['Golden rules',['say what you see','show you care','hear them out','know your role','connect to help']],['Listening',['active listening','validate','open questions','avoid','minimizing','secret']],['Getting help',['trusted adult','counselor','988','text','911','immediate danger','self-care']]],model:'A student who notices a friend withdrawing, skipping meals or posting hopeless messages can start by choosing a private, relaxed time and following the Be There golden rules. First, say what you see: “I’ve noticed you haven’t been coming to practice and seem really down.” Second, show you care: “I care about you and I’m here for you.” Third, hear them out by listening actively—giving full attention, reflecting back what they say, asking open questions like “What has that been like?” and validating their feelings with phrases like “That sounds really hard,” while avoiding minimizing, comparing or rushing to give advice. Fourth, know your role: a friend can listen and encourage but cannot diagnose or treat, and should not be someone’s only support. Fifth, connect to help: encourage them to talk to a trusted adult such as a parent, coach or school counselor, and offer to go with them. If they mention thoughts of suicide or self-harm, I would ask directly whether they are thinking about suicide, not promise to keep it secret and immediately tell a trusted adult or help them call or text 988. If they are in immediate danger, I would call 911. Afterward, I would check in again and take care of myself by talking with my own trusted adult.'},sources:[RULES,BE_THERE,JED,LIFELINE]};

export const MH5={title:'Needs assessment and working with your Mental Health Consultant',hook:'Judges want to know why your team chose its topic. “It seemed interesting” earns fewer points than “Our counselor told us that a third of freshmen feel they have no adult to talk to.” A needs assessment and a strong partnership with your Mental Health Consultant make your campaign focused, safe and convincing.',objectives:['Explain the role and selection of a Mental Health Consultant.','Complete the Mental Health Consultation Form correctly.','Conduct a simple, ethical needs assessment of your school or community.','Write a clear campaign goal and measurable objectives.'],sections:[
['The Mental Health Consultant',[
'Every team identifies a **Mental Health Consultant**: a **school mental health professional** (counselor, nurse, psychologist or social worker) or a **community mental health professional**. If none is available, the **local HOSA advisor** may serve. The consultant provides **oversight of content** and **support and guidance**. Teams decide how many meetings to hold and may work with more than one consultant (uploading a form for each).',
'Use your consultant to choose an appropriate topic and risk or protective factors, review posts for accuracy and safety, confirm school resources and help you respond if a peer reaches out after seeing the campaign. In Round Two, judges award points for explaining **how the consultant was used in the learning process**, so keep notes from every meeting.',
"Consultants are busy, so respect their time. Send an agenda before each meeting, arrive prepared and on time, and send a short thank-you note afterward. Professional conduct is one of the objectives the consultant rates on the form, and a good working relationship can continue after the competition.",
match('Consultant task and why it matters',[['Approve the topic','Ensures it fits student needs'],['Review posts','Checks accuracy and safety'],['Confirm resources','Keeps contact information current'],['Plan responses','Prepares for peers who reach out'],['Sign the form','Required for the upload'],['Share feedback','Improves the team’s work']])]],
['The Mental Health Consultation Form',[
'The form records the **competitors’ names**, **school**, **HOSA advisor**, **consultant’s name and title**, **dates of consultation**, **total time spent** meeting, **topics discussed** and the **consultant’s signature**. The consultant also rates the team on five objectives: explaining HOSA, background knowledge of school or community needs, background knowledge of mental health, contributing ideas and being receptive to suggestions about seeking help, and professional conduct.',
'Prepare for meetings: explain what HOSA is and what the event requires, bring your research and questions, take notes and follow up with thanks. The completed form must be **inserted as an image** in your summary presentation and is worth **10 points** in Round One—an incomplete or missing form earns zero for that item.',
order('Preparing for a consultant meeting',['Schedule a time that works for the consultant','Explain HOSA and the event','Share your research and proposed topic','Ask focused questions','Take notes on advice','Follow up with thanks and next steps'])]],
['Conducting a needs assessment',[
'A **needs assessment** identifies what students at your school or community actually face. Sources include **conversations with your consultant**, existing data such as the **school climate survey** or your state’s **Youth Risk Behavior Survey** results, national data from CDC, and **student input** through short, anonymous surveys or focus groups approved by your school.',
'Follow **ethical rules**: get **school permission** before surveying students, keep responses **anonymous**, avoid asking about personal experiences of self-harm, suicide or abuse, and never collect identifying information. Ask about **attitudes and needs** instead—for example, “Do you know how to schedule time with a counselor?” or “Which stress-relief strategies would you like to learn?”',
sort('Appropriate survey question?',['Appropriate','Not appropriate'],[['“Do you know how to reach the school counselor?”',0],['“Have you ever hurt yourself? Give details.”',1],['“Which coping strategies would you like to learn?”',0],['“List your name and your diagnosis.”',1],['“How connected do you feel to our school (1–5)?”',0]])]],
['Explaining your “why”',[
'In Round Two, the **“why”** criterion is worth **15 points**. Judges want details of the **needs assessment** and a clear, appealing reason for the topic. A strong “why” combines **data** (“Our counselor reported a rise in students missing class because of anxiety”), **evidence** (“CDC identifies school connectedness as a protective factor”) and **personal connection** (“We noticed friends struggling after transferring schools”).',
'Write a one-sentence **problem statement** and a one-sentence **campaign goal**. For example: “Many freshmen at our school feel disconnected (problem). Our campaign will strengthen school connectedness by helping freshmen find clubs and trusted adults (goal).” Keep personal stories general and respectful of privacy.',
chk('Which “why” statement is strongest?',['“Our counselor reported many freshmen feel isolated, and CDC identifies connectedness as protective, so we focused on clubs and trusted adults.”','“We thought it would be fun.”','“It was the first topic we found.”','“Mental health is important.”'],'Combine needs data, evidence and connection.')]],
['Setting goals and measurable objectives',[
'Turn your goal into **SMART objectives**: **S**pecific, **M**easurable, **A**chievable, **R**elevant and **T**ime-bound. For example: “By April 1, 60% of students who view our campaign will be able to name two ways to contact a counselor, measured by a short anonymous poll.” Objectives guide your content and give you results to share.',
'Measurement can include **reach and engagement** (views, shares, saves), **knowledge** (short polls before and after), **behavior intentions** (“I would talk to a trusted adult”) and **feedback** from your consultant. Use numbers carefully: engagement shows attention, not necessarily changed behavior.',
"Plan your measurement before you launch. Write the before-poll questions, decide when you will run the after-poll and agree on which numbers you will report. Measuring at the end without a starting point makes it hard to show change.",
order('Writing a SMART objective',['Name the audience','State the specific change','Make it measurable','Check it is achievable','Connect it to your factor','Set a deadline'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Planning with the consultant',{a:{text:'Your school counselor agrees to be your consultant. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you bring to the first meeting?',choices:[['An explanation of HOSA and the event, research and questions','b','Correct.',true],['A finished campaign to post that day','b','Plan with the consultant first.',false]]},b:{text:'You want to survey students. What does the counselor recommend?',choices:[['A short anonymous survey about attitudes and needs, approved by the school','c','Correct.',true],['A survey asking students to describe personal crises','c','Avoid sensitive personal questions.',false]]},c:{text:'You record the meeting date, time and topics on the consultation form and set SMART objectives.',end:true}})]],
["Using data responsibly",["Data make your “why” convincing, but use them carefully. **Cite** every number with its source and year. Use **state or national data** (such as YRBS) when school-level data are not available, and say so clearly. Do not exaggerate: “about 4 in 10 students nationally” is accurate; “almost every student at our school is depressed” is not.","Protect **privacy**. Never share results that could identify an individual, and avoid reporting results for very small groups. Do not single out groups of students in a way that could increase stigma; instead, present differences respectfully and focus on solutions.","Turn data into a **story**: one clear number, what it means for students at your school and how your campaign responds. Judges remember a single, well-explained statistic better than a list of ten.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Responsible use of data?","buckets":["Responsible","Not responsible"],"items":[["Citing the YRBS year and source",0],["Claiming “everyone at our school is depressed”",1],["Reporting only anonymous, grouped results",0],["Sharing a survey answer that identifies a student",1],["Explaining one key number clearly",0],["Listing many numbers with no explanation",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['The Mental Health Consultant (school or community mental health professional, or the HOSA advisor) oversees content and guides the team; the signed form with dates, time, topics and ratings is inserted as an image and is worth 10 points.','A needs assessment uses consultant input, school and YRBS data and approved anonymous surveys about attitudes and needs—never personal crises or identifying information.','The 15-point “why” combines needs data, evidence and connection; write a problem statement, a goal and SMART objectives measured by reach, knowledge polls and feedback.'],terms:[['Mental Health Consultant','A professional who oversees and guides the team’s campaign content.'],['Needs assessment','A process for identifying the needs of a school or community.'],['SMART objective','A specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound goal.']],mcqs:[q('Who may serve as the Mental Health Consultant?','A school counselor, nurse, psychologist or community mental health professional','A classmate','A social media influencer','No one is needed','Or the HOSA advisor.'),q('How is the consultation form included in the upload?','As an image in the summary presentation','As a separate mailed copy','It is not included','As a video','Required item.'),q('Which survey question is appropriate?','“Do you know how to reach the school counselor?”','“Describe your last crisis.”','“Write your name and diagnosis.”','“Have you hurt yourself?”','Attitudes and needs.'),q('How many points is the Round Two “why” criterion worth?','15','2','50','100','Needs assessment and reason.'),q('What does the M in SMART stand for?','Measurable','Mental','Many','Medical','Objectives.'),q('Before surveying students, you should:','Get school permission and keep responses anonymous','Collect names','Ask about trauma','Post results publicly','Ethics.'),q('May a team work with more than one consultant?','Yes, uploading a form for each','No, never','Only at ILC','Only if they are relatives','Acceptable but not required.'),q('Engagement numbers like views show:','Attention, not necessarily behavior change','Proof of improved mental health','Diagnosis rates','Nothing','Use carefully.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would work with a Mental Health Consultant and conduct a needs assessment to choose and justify a campaign topic.',rubric:[['Consultant',['counselor','psychologist','nurse','advisor','oversight','guidance','review','meetings']],['Form',['dates','time','topics','signature','image','upload']],['Needs assessment',['data','yrbs','school climate','survey','anonymous','permission','attitudes']],['Justification',['why','problem statement','goal','evidence','smart','measurable']]],model:'Our team would ask our school counselor to be our Mental Health Consultant because counselors know student needs and can provide oversight of our content. At the first meeting, we would explain what HOSA is and what the event requires, share our early research and ask which youth topics and protective factors would be most helpful at our school. We would meet several times—to choose the topic, review draft posts for accuracy and safety, confirm resources such as how to book a counseling appointment and plan how to respond if a student reaches out after seeing a post. After each meeting we would record the date, time spent and topics discussed on the Mental Health Consultation Form, which the counselor would sign and rate, and we would insert it as an image in our summary presentation. For the needs assessment, we would review our school climate survey and state YRBS results with the counselor and, with school permission, run a short anonymous survey about attitudes and needs, such as whether students know how to reach a counselor and which coping strategies they want to learn, without asking about personal crises or collecting names. If the results showed that many freshmen feel disconnected, we would write a problem statement and goal around building school connectedness, support it with CDC evidence that connectedness is protective, and set a SMART objective such as having 60% of viewers able to name two ways to reach a counselor by April 1.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_YRBS,SG]};
